nd we talked about  caps  but to me the issue is not really caps  because both these leagues had caps its  minimum spending by teams by the league  I just don't think it's high enough in  the NFL the Union can work on that but  even greater need is the individual  agents especially for the Stars to  really hold the feet to the fire of the  teams to say we really need this  guaranteed beyond two years yeah I mean  what is they what other Raiders can I  say to Derek Carr and his agents when  they say you're really worried about  cutting them at age 30 why don't you  just guarantee it and those are the kind  of things you got to break the seal and  it has to start with the stars like a  car like a look like a Rodgers like a  Cam Newton and then you'll have a  trickle down after that that's what it's  going to take Ross speaking of breaking  the seal by the way I am going to wait  as long as possible tonight after I  start having some daddy sodas before I I  break the seal Andrew appreciate any  time you can come on and join me here  talking to Andrew Brandt the vice  president of player finance for the  Green Bay Packers for a very very long  time what about just the the total money  and you're like what about I know  there's less players in the NBA and I  know that there's a lot more games but  where's the NBA revenue at compared to  the NFL and how much bigger of a slice  to the NBA players get of the total  revenue than the NFL guys do and I do  hear the argument all the time when I  tweet about the difference in these  contracts but hey the NBA has got so  many fewer players obviously I know that  you know that everyone listening knows  that about a quarter of the roster so 15  players versus 60 when you kind of  practice squad injured players etc for  the NFL but look at it this way the cap  last year and the NBA was 94 million for  15 players so you're talking whatever  that is 66 points something million  average if you just slice it down that  way for the NFL you're talking 155  million for 60s sometimes 70 players  so that slice allocation is about two  million per player a little over two so  we're talking about three times the  difference allocation for NBA players  that just that doesn't seem right when  the revenues I don't know the most  recent NBA number Ross but you know the  NFL is going up about a billion dollars  a year in total revenues it dwarfs other  leagues so we're going to be about  fourteen billion by the end of next year  in terms of total revenue so revenue  should matter and it just doesn't seem  like they're mattering in this case so  what about when you look at the model  right now for the two sports I think  that's what we're getting to we're  getting to the heart of right model  between the NBA and the NFL the NBA is  generating it seems like Andrew a lot  more buzz in the offseason that maybe  even the NFL specifically free agency  okay I think the NFL Draft still gets  more than the NBA Draft but specifically  free agency there's just a lot more buzz  in the NBA because there can be these  big trades like the Chris Paul trade  doesn't seem to happen very often the  NFL and you've got some marquee players  that are free agent and likely to switch  teams which again rarely happens in the  NFL but the flip side I guess andrew is  you had a bunch of teams in the NBA that  really have no shot because they don't  have you know two or three star players  like you need which what do you think  has the better model you know I think it  depends on how you feel about management  and players there is more liberties  there's more power in the NBA player  base than the NFL player base and one  reason you've got to think if not for  obvious reasons the leadership of the  NBA Players Association the president is  a guy you've we've obviously been  talking about the past couple days Chris  Paul and the vice president is a guy  named LeBron James I think that matters  I really do I just think they bring a  gravitas to the bargaining table with  owners sitting across from them nothing  against the player leaders for the NFLPA  I just think if you had pick a name  Russell Wilson Cam Newton  Aaron Rodgers Tom Brady sitting in  across I think it would come out at the  end of the deal that there'd be a little  more given I just think that brings it  the other part of it is that the NBA  really has because of these future  guarantees the trading is interesting  because it's about expiring contracts  it's about picking up players that will  be off your roster like Brook Lopez  traded Lakers the headline was well  they'll use his cap room in 18 to get a  superstar and that's much more  interesting than sort of the NFL where  there are no future guarantees except  for a loan exception like a Brock  Osweiler trade you just don't even need  those trades because teams just dump  players because their contracts aren't  guaranteed so there's just a lot more  interesting business options when you  have players having the power that they  do and can dramatically alter fortunes  of teams last question I have for you in  Jersey and it's something that you and I  talked about it might even earlier this  week on on my podcast but the next thing  that's coming up that's going to be big  after NBA free agency after about the  next week or so it's going to be the  franchise tag deadline in the NFL and  it's really primarily going to be Kirk  Cousins that's more interesting than  lady on bail or Tremaine Johnson  whatever I'm just curious to get your  thoughts on what you think is going to  happen with Kirk Cousins because every  report you hear everybody out there says  it's highly highly unlikely that they  get a deal done yeah and I and I believe  you as well have a little bit of counter  intuitive feeling about Kirk Cousins I  hear about all this leverage and if they  get this franchise tag next year and  35-minute whatever I just think deadline  spur action it's going to come down to a  situ